WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.080
Let’s be honest:

00:00:07.430 --> 00:00:09.380
You could get doored.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.980
Or hit by a bus.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.389
If you were a cyclist in new york city, in
the 2000s,

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:20.080
it was almost like you were a cast member
from Escape From New York

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.580
Maybe if you were an extreme sports person,
it was fantastic for you

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.409
Because you were dodging cars,

00:00:28.409 --> 00:00:35.559
It was not a place to have any kind of safe
commuting regular transport experience

00:00:35.559 --> 00:00:40.809
Today, New York City is seeing explosive growth
in cycle commutes.

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:48.929
So, how did a city with traffic like this
get so many people biking downtown?

00:00:48.929 --> 00:00:52.129
[Vox]

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:57.500
From 2007 to 2013, Janette Sadik-Khan led
New York City’s Department of Transportation.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:01.940
I was responsible for 6000 miles of street,

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.739
789 bridges,

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:05.910
12000 signalized intersections,

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.610
1.3 million street signs,

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:14.020
We put down 400 miles of on street bike lanes, including one of the first parking protected

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:18.000
bike lanes in the united states,  and we did it on 9th Avenue

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Between 23rd street and 16th street.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.790
This lane was significant, because it was
an early parking protected design.

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:25.790
It's much less stressful than conventional lanes.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.080
But installing it was quite controversial.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.670
Because making the space for protected lanes meant the elimination of

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.350
parking.

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:40.770
Taking away parking spaces is not for the
faint of heart.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:46.610
And there is no super secret magic recipe
that’s going to make it easier to do.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:51.960
But you need to make a case about what you are trying to do, right?

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.140
The 9th Avenue pilot ran for 6 blocks

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.270
And if you look at the blueprint for the street,

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.110
You’ll notice an alternating pattern

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:04.230
On blocks with right-hand turns, there’s
an entire lane of parking.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:10.100
But at left-turn turns, those parking spots are sacrificed for a barrier

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.200
The vehicles will wait next to the bike lane
here,

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.200
And separate signals for cars and bikes stagger,

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.200
to reduce stress at the intersection.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:23.590
Data show that the 9th Avenue bike lane
produced economic, mobility, and safety benefits.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.660
we saw crashes go down, some 47%.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:31.340
retail sales went up almost 49%

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:35.250
Cars had dedicated turn lanes, so the traffic processed much better.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.810
And bikers got a dedicated lane.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:40.770
So it was a win for business, it was a win
for drivers, it was a win for people on foot.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.980
And it was a win for people on two wheels.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.010
And that really set the stage for all that
followed.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.700
Once the city analyzed data from the 9th Avenue pilot,

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.700
the miles of protected lane in New York skyrocketed.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:57.770
By 2018, the city had nearly 1200 miles of bike lane.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:01.130
And 100 of those were protected.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:06.209
Because we weren’t going to accommodate a million more people by double decking our

00:03:06.209 --> 00:03:07.810
streets and highways.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:12.650
And so designing streets for people, that
make it easier to bike, easier to walk,

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.980
and easier to take the bus — that’s the kind of recipe for future success of cities.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:22.510
the cities that make these kinds of investments and changes are the cities that are growing and

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.210
thriving in this century.

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:30.430
But to really draw a crowd of cyclists, a
city needs a network of low-stress bike lanes.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:34.490
you can’t just paint sharrows on a street
and expect that people are going to voila, start biking.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:40.270
It needs to be a reliable system, and it needs to be safe.

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:46.400
The way that we look at the health of bike
lanes, and our bike lane network, is how many

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:49.980
women and children are using the lanes.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.819
When you see women and children in the lanes, and families in the lane, you know that it’s

00:03:53.819 --> 00:03:54.819
safe.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:00.300
Families, among other individuals, would fall into the ‘interested, but
concerned’ group on this chart.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.780
It’s from one survey taken in Oregon.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:07.480
But consensus in the transportation field
suggests that this group is the untapped potential

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.610
for cities who want to promote cycling.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:14.040
Which is the big factor behind New York’s
boom in urban cycling.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.112
And it was pretty cost effective, too.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.819
You know, the bike lanes were like 99%
of our headlines, but they were only 1% of

00:04:22.819 --> 00:04:23.819
the budget.

00:04:23.819 --> 00:04:25.270
I don’t think there’s a better investment.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:28.239
If you want to build a better city, you can
start by building better bike lanes.

